Yes! **[rust-phf](https://github.com/rust-phf/rust-phf)** is the Rust equivalent - compile-time perfect hash maps.

**Integration approach for descent:**

```
|keywords :fallback /bare_string
| true   => BoolTrue
| false  => BoolFalse
| null   => Nil
| nil    => Nil
```

**Generates:**

```rust
use phf::phf_map;

// Compile-time perfect hash - O(1) lookup
static BARE_KEYWORDS: phf::Map<&'static [u8], fn(&mut Parser<'_>, &mut impl FnMut(Event))> = phf_map! {
b"true" => Parser::emit_bool_true,
b"false" => Parser::emit_bool_false,
b"null" => Parser::emit_nil,
b"nil" => Parser::emit_nil,
};

fn parse_bare_value(&mut self, on_event: &mut F) {
let content = self.term();
if let Some(emitter) = BARE_KEYWORDS.get(content) {
emitter(self, on_event);
} else {
self.emit_bare_string(content, on_event);
}
}
```

**Benefits:**
- Single probe lookup (no state machine)
- Works directly on `&[u8]` - no UTF-8 conversion
- Scales to large keyword sets
- Compile-time generated, zero runtime cost

**For UDON specifically:**

The current pattern (dispatch on first char, then state-per-char) is actually trying to do what phf does automatically. With phf, you'd just:

1. Accumulate the bare value content
2. Single lookup in the keyword map
3. Emit appropriate event or fall back to BareValue

Want me to add this to the TODO as a potential feature? It would eliminate all the `kw_true`, `kw_false`, `kw_null` boilerplate.
